INT: What can you tell us about relationships between Jews and non-Jews in your childhood?

VK: Well, in my childhood, most of my friends were Jewish and they were in Shanghai. Because my childhood was spent in Shanghai, China. And we all went to the same school. And most of the people were from Austria and Germany. So they were mostly Jews.

INT: I'm going to interrupt you for just a minute. We're going to get to China in a minute. Do you have any memories from Vienna as a child?

VK: No, I was two years old when I left.

INT: So you have no memory of the Anschluss or anything like that?

VK: No, no.

INT: When you think back to the time before you left Vienna, nothing sticks out in your mind at all? No childhood home?

VK: No, only the stories that I was told by my parents. I really don't remember anything.

INT: What prompted your parents' decision to leave Austria?

VK: Well, my parents had a liquor business. And there was a Nazi collecting all their money, sitting there and collecting any money that came into the business. And we escaped on the High Holy Days.

INT: What year was this?

VK: In 1938.

INT: So this was before Kristallnacht?

VK: Just before Kristallnacht, but my family's-- many members of my family who came to China later were there still for Kristallnacht.

