INT: As you have said, you were only two.

VK: I was two years old, but I was told that I fell into a bucket of water, and they didn't have any change for me. [LAUGHING] Because I was walking already. Yes, that's what I was told.

INT: Do you have any memories of your first impression of Shanghai?

VK: Oh, definitely remember first impression, not when I arrived, but my first impressions, yes.

INT: Please share them.

VK: OK. Well, I do know that I do remember having-- living in-- outside of the ghetto. And we had to move, once we were interned, into the other place. When the Japanese took over, we were interned, and we had to move inside. And the conditions were very poor. Once I was in interned, we-- I had an outhouse. I never had a bathtub. I never saw a bathtub the whole time I lived in China. I never had a bathtub. We had a bucket of water. And our drinking water, we had to boil. And it was all boiled on a coal stove. We had a little coal stove. There was no electricity like that, or anything like that. It was-- the conditions were very, very poor. It was a very poor section once we were interned. Even when we were outside, I never had a bathtub. But I did have a flush toilet when we were living on the-- before we were interned. We had a flush toilet. But when we were interned, we lived in a place where we had-- I had to go downstairs to an outhouse as a child. So that I remember very well. Even if it was in the middle of the night, I had to get dressed because it was cold in the winter. It was cold in the winter, and it was very hot in the summer.

INT: Can you tell us where you first settled before you were interned?

VK: We first settled on Wayside Road, which was right outside of the thing, and it had a park across the street. There was a park across the street.

INT: Do you remember the living conditions in that first place, other than you had a flush toilet?

VK: It was-- we were all always in one room. We couldn't afford anything too much because we didn't have-- we had-- we needed assistance and the food supply was not the best in that case. But when I was five years old, I started to go to school, which was founded by the Kadoories and the Sassoons. They were very wealthy people. And we-- I started learning English when I was five years old. Before that, I spoke German, which we did in Austria and Germany. So I do speak both English and German.

INT: Did you learn any Chinese?

VK: No. The Chinese spoke in pidgin English or pidgin German, what you might call. Like, they had those things across their shoulder, and they were selling-- they were saying, like, things like porcelain, which is porcelain, In other words, they fixed porcelain and things like that, the Chinese. The Chinese, they acclimated to us. But we had Chinese friends on the streets, and we played with them on the street. [PAUSES] Children outside, downstairs, we played with other children, Chinese children outside. Yes. They were very friendly. We never had any problems with them, with the Chinese. [PAUSES] There was no antisemitism as far as I know, in China.

INT: What was the Jewish community in Shanghai like when you arrived?

VK: When I arrived, I don't know. Later on, I can tell you that they tried to make things like they did in Europe. They had concerts. They had a theater. They had a coffeehouse, a Vienna coffeehouse. And there, they had a piano. And-- but that was basically for my parents, you know? As children, we did things in the school, like plays and things like that, like you do here, you know, everything in school as a child. As a child, you didn't have the problems that the parents went through, the Holocaust, you know?

INT: Can you explain that a little more?

VK: Well, the parent-- our parents knew what was worrying them and they were concerned about different things and trying to make a living and all that. But the children, we didn't have those problems. We just played our games and went to school and did all these other things. So-- and you had friends. And you went and you did everything. You went to other people's houses, the street, other people's houses, other people's places to live. Most people only had one room. And it was everything. It was everything. They did-- it was their bedroom, their living room, their kitchen. It was all in one room.

INT: What about food and medical care in Shanghai?

VK: Well, we tried to get food. Some food was hard to get. For instance, I never liked milk because the milk there was awful. It tasted awful. So my whole life, I never liked milk.

There's some things you couldn't get. You couldn't get meat. Because that was hard to get. But we had potatoes and we made a-- I remember my mother making, like, a schmaltz, a spread with carrots and something else, camouflaging it. But we were not completely starved at any point. We had some food to get, but it wasn't always what we wanted to get.

INT: And medical care?

VK: And medical care, you had a place where you could go for medical care, you know, if you needed it, if you needed it.

INT: What about religious freedom and observing holidays in Shanghai?

VK: You had the religious-- all the religious freedom you wanted. There was a synagogue there, two synagogues, I believe. But one synagogue is called the Ohel, Ohel Synagogue, which is now used as a museum. And they tried to-- they tried to keep some of the Jewish things that were going on at the time. And there is a certain area that can be visited which is part of what the Jews in Shanghai went through. But a lot of it, a lot of the other parts were destroyed at a point. The poor section where I lived, that no longer exists. Because I went back there. As a reunion, we had lots of reunions everywhere. And I-- we went back there in 2006. I went back to China in the reunion-- as a reunion.

INT: We can talk about that a little bit later.

VK: OK.

INT: Did you observe any holidays in Shanghai?

VK: Yes.

INT: Any Jewish holidays?

VK: All of the Jewish holidays, we observed.

INT: How did your family support itself?

VK: Well, they tried many different things. My father had an orthopedic shop, orthopedic technician. And before we were interned, he was in a different place on Peking Road outside of the district. But then when we had to be interned, he had to move his business into the area where he was. So then he was on Tongshan Road there, which was not as good an area. And it was hard for him to make a living. But my mother made belts during the war. She made belts, braided belts. After the war was over, and we were able to go to the outside, my mother became-- when-- was a sales girl. She was a sales lady in a PX, and in other-- later on, in a very nice Chinese store. That was after the war was over, you know, after we were able to go in the outside again. But it was very hard. During the war, it was hard to make enough money. That's why we could not move into a better place, because we didn't have the money.

INT: A few moments ago, you had alluded to moving from one place to another--

