VK: OK. Well, as far as I can only remember is when I started school. And that was always very good. We had a very good education. We had gymnastics. And we had-- I had dancing for a while and a few other things. And that continued. The school continued. It was the same when-- even when after we moved into the intern. But we had to live in that ghetto area when the Japanese took over. And it was very, very crowded. And like I said, I moved into a place where I had an outhouse. And the conditions were bad.

But we still played with the same children and went to the same school, OK? The school was actually in the area-- in the internship area.

That's all I remember at this point about it. I had friends. I had many, many friends. And when we had reunions here, I went to many, quite a few reunions. I also went to reunions of my husband. My husband was also a Holocaust survivor. - We can talk about him a little bit later, too. [BACKGROUND NOISE]

INT: Can you tell us about when Japan got involved? What other changes, besides you physically having to move, what other changes were instituted?

VK: As far-- in my life, there were not too many changes except for the fact that if you wanted to go outside of the district, you had to ask, get permission from the Japanese. Because they didn't want you to work outside or anything. They really didn't like it unless it was an emergency. All I remember is I, as a child, had an emergency. I was bitten by a dog. And I had to get 18 rabies shots. So my father had to get permission from the Japanese to go outside because the doctors were not there to help us with that. A Japanese doctor took care of it. My father took me every day with the rickshaw to get my shots outside there. And of course, that didn't hurt so much. But the cleaning out the wound, that hurt. [LAUGHING] I remember that as a child. That's one of the things. And I remember having different things. I did have measles, mumps. I had those things. And one of my-- she was an aunt, an aunt of mine that was also in Shanghai. Like I said, there were more people there. And she came and played cards with me when I had the measles. And I remember those things.

All those people are gone. A lot of them lived in San Francisco. That's a lot where a lot of people also ended, in San Francisco. [PAUSES] Yeah. But that's about it. I was in San Francisco for a few years, too. Well, I'll tell you that later.

INT: During your time in Shanghai, you kind of alluded this-- to this a minute ago. In what ways did the Jewish community engage in self-help? How did Jews help each other in Shanghai?

VK: Well, if you needed help, you can always go to what was called a It was an organization where they-- a lot of people lived in that area that couldn't even afford to be in their own room. And they would help you. There was Jewish organizations called The Joint. Did you ever hear of it? Yes. OK. And there were many other organizations that would help you in that area. And they formed that place. You would also have to go there if you had an injury and you needed some help. They would help you. There was a hospital there. And one of my uncles was a male nurse in that hospital.

INT: You talked about the schools that were there. Who organized the schools?

VK: The schools were actually formed by two people who were very wealthy, the Kadoories and the Sassoons.

INT: And who were they?

VK: And they'd been in China for many years, were in the silk industry and a few other things. All I know is they became very, very wealthy. And they helped the Jewish people out, and also the Russian Jews that were there, that came to China in the '20s, who helped us out originally with everything else. But our schools were separate from their schools. And like I said, our school was formed by most of the people from Shanghai lived there, that were teachers there, et cetera.

INT: So the two families that set up the schools, were they Jewish families?

VK: I believe so. I don't really know. I don't know. I don't know all that much about them, actually.

INT: Is there anything else that stands out in your mind from the period of time before Pearl Harbor, in Shanghai?

VK: No, no. No, not that I can tell, that I mentioned.

INT: How did things change, then, with Pearl Harbor?

VK: Oh, yes! Once the Americans started bombing, they bombed China. Of course, we were rooting for the people, but we were hoping that we weren't getting hit. There was the one time when they hit the Hongkew area. Our area was called the Hongkew. But there were many people there. Of course, some of them died from the cholera and different things, older people that I know of, no children that I know. And I still had friends. But the bombing did not hurt me. I was taking piano lessons for four months. And I could only take it in a cafe where there was one piano. And the bombs were falling one time. That I remember. And my-- I continued playing the piano. That-- it didn't faze me at all as a child. And the piano started hopping away with me, would you believe this? Started hopping, and I was hopping with the piano. [LAUGHING] I only took--

INT: Because of the vibrations?

VK: Yes. I only take the piano lessons for a very short time. You know?

INT: Japan was allied with Nazi Germany. What pressures did the Germans try to impose?

VK: Well, they--

INT: --on the Japanese to get to the Jews? Do you-- were you aware of anything?

VK: Yes, yes, I know about it. They tried to pressure the Japanese to do away with the Jews like they did. But the Japanese said they are our Jews, and we will do what we want with them. And they would not listen to what the Germans wanted. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] They did not really bother us too much, at least not from my experience.

INT: Do you have any insight into why the Japanese were not more receptive to what the Germans were saying? Why did they not bother the Jews? Do you have any idea what was--

VK: Well--

INT: --the relationship between--

VK: --I don't think that they were antisemitic in any way. I didn't find any antisemitism in China, not before or after they took over. And they did not want to kill people. They did not want to do that.

INT: Were there any other changes under Japanese rule besides having to get permission to leave the area? Were there any changes in food or shelter or medical care?

VK: Well, we couldn't get things as much. And we couldn't make a living as easily. And we couldn't get certain foods. I remember that meat or certain things we couldn't get. Once in a while we got chicken, OK? But things were very expensive. But the Jewish people set up a delicatessen in the area. So if you had money, you could get that stuff. Depends how well you are. And we had everything. We had delicatessens and cafe houses, even restaurants. They set up even restaurants in the area, in the poor area. But all of this is gone now. It's no longer existing.

INT: When you look back to that time of your life, the war years in Shanghai, what would you like us to understand about what your life was like then?

VK: Well, all I can say is my-- you should never be forced to leave your country. You should never have to go through a Holocaust again. That's what I think for all future people. That's why it's so important to tell your story for future generations.

INT: But what should we understand about what life was like for the Jews in Shanghai? For somebody like me, who has never been, and, you know--

VK: It was a--

INT: What was the community like?

VK: It was a complete cultural change for my parents, totally, totally different culture. They came from a beautiful city where they had a wonderful life and were completely uprooted and had to start all over in a-- which was a completely different way of life. It was-- it was very difficult for them. As a child, it didn't bother me. But for them, it was-- and I didn't even know the difference. I didn't know anything but China from the age I was till I left it, till I left China. My whole childhood, that was there.

INT: How much did the Jewish community in Shanghai know about what was happening to the Jews that were left behind under the Nazis?

VK: Well, they didn't know until-- I remember that until I was a little older, they had put up signs of people who were alive and who were killed. And I know that my grandmother lost her-- one of her brothers. One of her brothers was killed. And it just so happens in my-- from my-- from that grandmother, she had other brothers and things that a lot of them left and went into different parts of the world. And as a result. Of her three-- of her brothers, I have lots of-- I had lots of relatives all over the world now. And I even found-- we even found, in Czechoslovakia, there were graves there of that town that she was born in, in Trebic, which is about half an hour away from Prague.

My great-great grandparents' graves are there. There were no Jews living in that town. And one of those cousins going three generations down, that lives in Vancouver, Canada, wrote a thick book about the family. We had a reunion in Prague, one reunion I went to, there were many from that family. I might have, maybe, 800 relatives. They're gone already. They died because one was in Sweden. My mother had one in London, in France. And one in France is still living, that I'm in touch with. My grandmother's maiden name was I'll go into that if you want it. And so I have a history on my grandmother's side. On my father's side, I have no history. Because the three brothers were in Shanghai, and the sister, but the grandparents died in Shanghai. My paternal grandparents died in Shanghai. And they have-- I only remember going to my grandmother on Friday nights and my one cousin went on Saturday morning to her, visiting her. And I have a picture of her and me. She died when I was about six years old. So-- and they buried her in Shanghai. But during the Cultural Revolution in the '60s, they overturned everything, and the graves are no longer there. They built over those graves and things. And we don't know what happened to those graves anymore. They found some graves of people in Shanghai, but not my grandparent. It's gone.

INT: I want to just get you to clarify one thing. You were talking about lists of people who had lived or who had died. Was this during the war or after the war?

VK: My grandparents died before the war.

INT: No, no, the lists, you mentioned lists of-- I had asked you about.

VK: Oh, the lists that they found of some-- they had some lists there that were put up by-- I don't know what organization. And that's how my grandmother found out that one of her brothers was killed. The other--

INT: Was this during or after the war?

VK: It was during the war. It was during the war. That was before the Japanese came into-- before the Japanese came. I believe it was before the Japanese took over. The Japanese took over in 1943, I believe it was, in '43. [PAUSES] At that time, I was seven years old. All right? So I do remember a few little things because my grandmother, I remember. And she died when I was six.

INT: Were you aware, in Shanghai, about the bombs going off in Hiroshima and Nagasaki?

VK: No, no. We learned about that later.

INT: When and how did you realize that the war in Europe was over? Because the war in Europe ended first. How did you find out about that?

VK: The war in Europe? Well, we knew that things were bad when we left. And my grandmother, who was there, saw the Kristallnacht as well as my aunt, my one aunt. And my mother had two brothers. And one of them went to Israel. I mean, I'm sorry. One of them went to-- was able to go to United States when we were in China. And the other brother was a member of the Hakoah. And he ended up in Buenos Aires, Argentina. So my mother had one brother in Buenos Aires in Argentina and one brother in the United States. And she came after Kristallnacht. They came to the United States. They were able to come because his wife had relatives here in the United States. So they were able to get to the United States fast. We had applied for-- from what I understand, to come to the United States. But it was taking too long. We never would have made it. So that's why we ended up going to China.

INT: Can you explain what Hakoah was? Hakoah, you mentioned Hakoah. What--

VK: Hakoah? Hakoah was a football and soccer team that he was with. And he ended up living all his life in Argentina. I saw him once when we came to New York. He came from Argentina. I saw that uncle, my uncle once. And I have one cousin there in Argentina. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] But in New York, in that-- my uncle that lived in New York, he had four children here. And so I have four cousins that are still living here. They're all younger than me. I'm the oldest now. And they're my only cousins left in the family. And that's on my maternal side. On my paternal side, there's nobody left. Because the one cousin that was in Shanghai with me, my father's brother, Isaac, they were all in Shanghai with us, my father's brothers. He passed away a year ago. He lived here in New York, too. He was in Israel first, and he went to Israel two years before we left China. Went to Israel, was in the Israeli navy, then came to the United States and was in the US Army. And then he went to school and became an engineer, and he worked for IBM. And he had one son. And he lived here in New York, in the New York area.

INT: Let's go back to Shanghai. [PAUSES] What do you remember about the last few months of the war?

VK: The last few months of the war? I only remember that when the war was over-- that I remember distinctly. There was a big, big going on there. And the Americans came in with their trucks and gave us all Hershey chocolates. So I do remember that distinctly. [LAUGHING] Then-- I was only-- let's see, how old was I when the war was over in 1945? Yeah, I was nine years old. All right? [BACKGROUND NOISE]

INT: What was the post-war period like in Shanghai? The Japanese are gone. What happened immediately after the Japanese left? Before-- maybe even before the Americans came in?

VK: Well, we were able to go anywhere in Shanghai, for one. And in 1948, my school disbanded because a lot of people left, either for Israel, United States, Canada. We were not-- we did not get our visa to get the United States. Even when we left on the General Gordon, we went to Canada first. That's what I'm trying to say. That's why I call my traveling suitcase. I have a thing here I went to Canada first. We came-- we left on the-- you want to have that story? We left on the General Gordon. No, you're not? You're not at that point?

INT: I'm not there quite yet. You're a little faster than I am. [LAUGHING]

VK: OK.

INT: Just a little faster than I am. You started to allude to something that I wanted to ask you about. How did the Jewish community in Shanghai change, starting in 1945 with the end of the war?

VK: Well, first of all, after our big celebrations, everybody was thinking about where are they going to go. Because we were able to leave Shanghai. Where are we able to go? We couldn't all go to the United States. They wouldn't let us in. You know, it took a long time, still then, to come to the United States. Some of them were able to come. There were quotas. They called it quotas. And the German quota was better than the Austrian quota. All right? I'm an Austrian. I'm Austrian quota. They were always able to leave earlier. It was a better quota. And also, it depended if you had family, and what family you had, and so forth, and many other things. And you had to be in perfect health. And you had to have-- make sure that you were not a burden to the United States. And now, people come rolling in there where they don't even do anything, right? They can come in on the borders. It's forget it. It's ridiculous. But anyway, I'm not going to even go into that. [LAUGHING] But anyway, what we had to go through to get here is another story. So we had to go to Canada first, because the communists were there already when we left. I left very late. I left in-- we left in November or September-- September or November, I'm not sure, of 1949. And it was the last ship that General Gordon took out of Shanghai. And I had to change schools because my school was disbanded, because most people left already. Like I said, my cousin went to Israel with his family, my father's brother. And my other uncle went to Australia, my youngest one, Jacob, went to Australia. Abraham, Isaac, and my father was Abraham. But my mother only wanted to go to the United States, not the pioneer country that Israel was, because she had her mother, who left two years before us from Shanghai to go to her son. He was-- she was able to go to the United States two years before. She had her mother in New York and her brother in New York. So my mother only wanted to come here. So we had entrance to the-- to Canada. So we went to Canada. So the ship we went on stopped in Japan, Yokohama, and in Hong Kong. And we were able to get off in those places.

