VK: Yep Yep.

INT: OK. Sorry. We had an internet problem here. We're back. I would like you to tell me, please, about-- you know, you mentioned the communists came, and then you changed the subject. Let's stay with the communists for a little bit. What was that like when they came in?

VK: Well, they came in. But really, they didn't bother us too much. We saw-- there wasn't much of a commotion. They really took over very easily. You know? There wasn't that much of a problem. And we were able to go, continue and go wherever we wanted. The only thing that they required, if we were to leave, is we needed to get three citizens to tell us that we do not owe any money-- any to anybody, and that we had no problem leaving the country, that everything was clear. And I as a child went to get it from our tailor, and from the guy who made my-- who made different things. And I got it from different people, the shoemaker, and so forth. And I was able to do that, and I got the paper saying that everything was all right. But I had to go to another school for nine months, from January to September. Yes, in September 1949 is when we left. I went to another school, which was in the other area of town. But it was called the Shanghai Jewish school. And-- but I did have four months of Chinese, which I never had. Because in my grade, the lower grades did not have to have Chinese or Japanese. But my cousin, who was four years older than I, almost five years older than I, he had all those languages in school. He's the one that went to Israel and was in the Israeli Navy, my one cousin who was in Shanghai with me, my one paternal cousin. And then he came to the United States after. But I will explain that later. Right? My school was terrific also. I had very good grades. And I got a very good report card from them when I left, when I told them I was leaving. So then we went on the boat. Do you want to continue that? No.

INT: No, not yet.

VK: Not yet. OK. [LAUGHING]

INT: Thank you, but not yet. Was there any increase of things like antisemitism or anything like that under communism?

VK: Not at the time that I know of, that I'm aware of, not that I'm aware of.

INT: So I realize that were a baby when you lived under Nazi rule. But you lived under the Germans, the Japanese, and then the Chinese communists. Can you give us some insight into any of those kinds of living situations and how they might have been different for you?

VK: No. The only thing that was different is when we had to move into the ghetto. And when the war was over, we could go out wherever we wanted, which was terrific at the time.

INT: Anything about China that you haven't told us about that you would like to share?

VK: All I know is that when everybody had really left and we were in China, at one time, for the first time, we took a trip-- China, to a resort, was a very nice resort. Then I remember that very distinctly. It had a pagoda. And I understand that Chiang Kai-Shek used to go there. That was a very nice resort. We went to a resort once before I left Shanghai. But most people were not aware of that anymore, because they left. They had already left. And of course, the school I went to, it was very different. I kind of missed some of the friends that had left. There were very few people left when we-- when I was in China at that time. And at that time, I was already 12 years old.

INT: One last question about China, and then we'll go on. OK? When you look back at all the years that you spent in China, how would you say you were treated by the Chinese people?

VK: We were treated good by the Chinese people. They didn't-- they did not bother us that much in any way. It's just that we had to live in the poorest section, which were really the slums. It was really very bad air. But I did go to Hebrew school at one point. I went to Bais Yaakov, yeah, when I was very young, when I was about seven years old, when we were in the-- interned already in the slums. And [PAUSES] it was not exactly how I lived at home. And you're very impressionable when you're seven years old, you know? There they didn't do much with the girls, OK? And they told us this is a sin, and that's a sin, and then I went home to my mother and said, you're a sinner. And I won't eat here anymore. And came Passover, I didn't want to go home. I stayed at that synagogue. Because they were Orthodox, and my mother certainly wasn't. [LAUGHING] Bais Yaakov is an Orthodox-- they didn't have that in the others. So I went through Orthodox, conservative, reform. I did-- so I went through everything in my life. Let's put it that way. But I did learn Yiddish there. Because I speak German, and Yiddish is very much like it. So I did pick up Yiddish, not Hebrew, but Yiddish. [LAUGHING]

INT: Now let's take the voyage out of China. Let's go step by step. You took-- you boarded the ship. And?

VK: I boarded the ship. The ship was called the General Gordon. And it was the last ship, last time they took the voyage out of China. They went to China a few times and took people back and forth. And it was in September 1949. And of course, we did not have clearance to come to the United States yet. So we stopped in Japan, in Yokohama, where we were able to get off and see part of Japan. And then we went to Hong Kong, which we saw. And from there on, we went to Hawaii. And my mother had tears in her eyes because they would not let us off in Hawaii because we were not going to the United States yet. We didn't have clearance to come in the United States. And from Hawaii, we went to San Francisco, ended up this is our last stop, San Francisco. And in San Francisco, my aunt, my father's sister was already in San Francisco. So they came aboard the ship with all kinds of cold cuts and things. And it was terrific. [LAUGHING] And they were in San Francisco. And of course, I loved it on the boat. I was at the time 13 years old. When I left, I was 13. So it was-- I enjoyed it. Everybody was seasick. I was not. I don't know. Everybody really was seasick. And the ship accommodations were pretty good. I mean, I really enjoyed it. And that-- now I know where I was going, but there I knew I was going. When I left Vienna, I was only two years old. I didn't know anything, you know? And I enjoyed the trip. And after that, we were taken by train all through Canada. We saw Vancouver and other areas. We took the train all the United States and all through Canada. So I did really see a lot. But my mother wanted to end up in Montreal, only because she wanted to go to her mother and her brother, who were in New York. So that was the closest place. And they came to visit us, my uncle and my grandmother. And I didn't see her since Shanghai, my grandmother. But my uncle, I didn't see at all. I never saw before-- and my two cousins, the older boys, the third boy, and my aunt just had a baby, the third boy. She had the third boy. She wanted a girl. She did have a girl after that. But we were in the United States many years later, and they all live here. And I'm the closest to the girl, who is really closer in age to my daughter than to me. So I have four cousins. My uncle passed away when he was about 65, I think. But my aunt-in-law, she lived to be 101. The mother, she lived to be 101. She only passed away a few years ago. OK? And anyway, so they-- she was in Vienna during Kristallnacht, but they came to the United States. They came to the United States. So I have my four cousins here. And one of her-- one of the grand-- one of my grand cousins got married. And I went to the wedding. It's better than the different funerals. Her mother, my-- passed away. That was last year, a year ago, right, a year ago. It's nice to see my four cousins once. My daughter couldn't come. My granddaughter took me to the wedding. My granddaughter is now 27. [LAUGHING] So I have my four cousins here. That was the story. [PAUSES] You want to tell--

INT: I wanted to ask you, again, you're running ahead of me just a little bit, what were your expectations of Canada? And what were your first impressions of Canada?

VK: Well, [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] Montreal is a very beautiful city. However, I was not happy there. [LAUGHING] I was new. I was an excellent student. But the French was crammed down my throat because it's English and French. And in the winter, it was cold. And everything had steps. And I used to fly down those steps, I don't know how many times. But never mind that.

I didn't like it in the school. I didn't have any friends. And I was new there. And the teacher didn't like the fact that I was a better student than the French people there. And-- but I went to that school because it was within walking distance from where I lived. And it was a middle school. All right? It was a middle school.

I lived there a year, a little over a year when the papers came that we can enter the United States. So my teacher didn't want to let me go. Outside-- she says-- I says but I have to leave. I was very upset at the time. So I went to her desk, put my books on her desk, and went to the principal's office and told him I had to leave. And I had the paper here that I had to leave. And he gave me a beautiful certification and report and told me to leave. And when we-- and when I came to New York, I had a wonderful reception. All the people-- the kids were all so friendly. Well, first of all, everybody-- I was-- we were put in the Lower East Side. The Lower East Side, my mother didn't like it. My mother liked Canada better. She didn't like it whatsoever. But I loved it. I had a wonderful time. Everybody was friendly. I had a lot of friends. First of all, I had a lot of Jewish people. When I was in Canada in school, I didn't have any Jewish kids. It was not a very Jewish school. That was OK. And I made friends so fast. But I was only here four months. And I graduated. They increase-- they put me in another grade. I graduated junior high school after being in New York for four months, PS 188. And I lived on East Sixth Street between B and C. And I liked it, and my mother absolutely hated it. Because that apartment, that apartment was a horror. That apartment, she says, I almost lived better in China. Oh, I forgot to tell you one important thing about Montreal. The first thing about Montreal was the bathtub. The one thing I loved, I saw for the first time a bathtub. And I was in the bathtub for a whole day, 24 hours, and they couldn't get me out. That's one thing about Montreal I liked. And also I was in the foot of Montreal, on St. Lawrence Street. And they had swings there. And I-- and it was a very pretty area, and I used to go on the swings there all by myself all the time. But in school, I was not happy in this school. I was a good student, but I wasn't happy in the school there. Anyway, but in New York, I was very happy. But my mother wasn't. [LAUGHING]

INT: Let me ask you just one or two quick questions about Canada.

Did you experience any antisemitism in Canada, or how were you treated by the Canadians?

VK: I personally did not. I don't know if there was some, but I did not experience anything, not at the time, anyway.

INT: You mentioned that you were the only Jewish student in this French school. That's why I was asking. Did you experience any anti-foreigner sentiment, either in Canada or in the United States?

VK: No. United States came later, after I left New York. I got to tell you that. There I had some strange experiences, but that's another thing. [PAUSES] In Canada, I did not feel that at all. No. I can only say I didn't like the winters. And I spent two winters there and one summer.

And the school, so I didn't really have any friends there, let's put it that way. That was one of my main reasons. I do remember one thing. I had one friend that came a little later from Shanghai, also Germany. She was a little older than me. I met her a couple of times in Canada. And she was also at the reunion that I once went-- Shanghai reunion later. Anyway, the two of us went-- because in Canada, you could not go to the movies until you were 16. OK? They had a very strict rule about the movies for children under 16. And so I got dressed up as if I was older, and she was older, too, so we were able to go to the movies. The only kind of movies they had were children's movies. And I wasn't interested in those. OK? I was 13 at the time I'm going, 14 at the time. All right. Anyway, so that's one thing I did. And of course, the bathtub, that was one thing. We didn't have TV or anything, but I had a radio, OK? I had a radio. So I listened to the radio a lot in Canada.

INT: While you were in Canada, were you connected at all with any Jews or any other refugees, survivors, anybody like that?

VK: Yeah, my-- yeah, some people that came to Canada that were in Montreal from Shanghai. I know my mother met some friends from Shanghai. Yeah. And there was an old teacher that was in Shanghai that was also in Montreal at the time. But like I said-- and that one girl, one girl I met was from Shanghai. And we were friends outside of class, but she just came--

INT: What about some--

VK: --at the end in Canada.

INT: I'm sorry.

VK: What?

INT:

VK: I think I'm frozen again.

INT: I can still hear you. It's OK. There you are. Did you meet any survivors, any Jewish survivors from Europe in Canada?

VK: Yes, that one gal that I told you about. And my mother had some friends that were there, they were Jewish survivors in Canada. And the teacher that I had, he was a Jewish survivor. They were.

INT: I'm just wondering what you might have heard from survivors from Europe of what they went through. Did you meet anybody from that had gone through the war in Europe and come to either Canada or later to the United States?

VK: The only ones I met were people that were survivors that were in Shanghai. That's the only ones I know about. There were other survivors that went in Montreal. Yes, there were. There were other survivors that were in Montreal. My mother knew a couple of people that were survivors that spoke German, also. You know, most of them that spoke German? [PAUSES] They used to speak German, and I didn't like it when I was here in this country or anywhere else. I didn't like it, that they spoke German. [LAUGHING] OK?

INT: Before you came to the United States, what were your expectations of America?

VK: Well, I can tell you, expectations are usually the movies, I tell you that, the movies. And when I left Shanghai on the boat, I came with a book and movie screens that I had pasted in. I knew all the movie stars, all right? That was what I brought back. And the money at that time, the Shanghai money? I threw it overboard. Because I'll tell you, $1 was like 1,000 yen. You needed-- for $1, from $1 US dollar at that time, which is now-- [LAUGHING] --it was, 1,000 yen. That's how much the dollar was worth at that time. And that the yen were nothing, so I threw them overboard [LAUGHING] at that time. Now it's a different story.

INT: So you shared with us your expectations of the United States. What were your first impressions of the United States when you got here?

VK: Well, I loved it. I just loved it. [LAUGHING] I was very happy to be here.

INT: How did you adjust to school, or social life, or anything in the United States? Was it easier than Canada?

VK: Oh, definitely it was easier than Canada to adjust.

But like I said, I was in New York only four months and graduated junior high school. And then I left for San Francisco, California, because my mother wanted to go to San Francisco, with my grandmother. My grandmother also came with us, OK, my mother's mother. My uncle stayed here. He lived here. And my mother was very happy in San Francisco. She found a very nice job as a sales lady. And I spent the summer still in New York with my grandmother and all. And then my grandmother and I took the train-- I took the train again. I saw the United States many times all the way to California. And I entered school in San Francisco. Of course, it was all new again. Now, here, I'll tell you about something very interesting. I met a gal who came from Germany. OK? She was also brand new. And we became best friends. She was not Jewish. She was German. And we had something in common. We both spoke German and English. And we were new in this school. And we were best friends. And to this day, it is-- now we're 88 years old. I'm still in touch with her. I call her once a week. She lives in San Francisco. And I went back to New York. Well, that-- let me get finished with San Francisco. The school was very, very nice. I was very happy there. I was a cheerleader. I got involved, and everything else. And I enjoyed my life. Of course, there I was very mixed with-- between Jewish people and a lot of other people. And I had a lot of friends there, like I said, mixed. In New York, it was mostly Jewish-- old Jews mostly. Lower East Side was very, very Jewish at that time. Anyways, so-- and our apartment, it was a horrible apartment. So my mother didn't like that. That's the apartment my aunt had gotten for me, probably because the rent, you know, we couldn't pay such a high rent. We were not, you know, we were new. At that time, things weren't handed to you now, like they give now everybody everything. It's a ridiculous thing. [LAUGHING] Anyway, so-- but in San Francisco, my mother was a salesgirl. And we did very, very well. And my father passed away. So that was the end of my dad.

INT: When did he pass away?

VK: In 19-- what was it, in 19-- I don't remember exactly, '62, I think it was, something like that, yeah.

INT: In San Francisco or in New York?

VK: No, in New York. He passed away in New York.

INT: I wanted to ask you what Jewish traditions you observed after the war when you came to the United States, in New York or in San Francisco? Did you still observe the way you did before?

VK: Yes, yes. I followed all Jewish traditions. We weren't kosher or anything like that. But the Jewish holidays and going to synagogue, yes. I didn't go regularly in San Francisco, no. My mother didn't go every Friday night to synagogue or anything like that, no. Here I do. I did. My husband and I did, because my husband came from a more religious family, OK, originally. And-- but we did not join a-- when we moved to Pearl River, we joined the temple. We formed a temple in Pearl River, which is not a Jewish area. We were very involved in it. And I've been a member. We've been a member. I'm still a member there, 55 years. And I was bar mitzvahed there. My children were bar mitzvahed in the temple there and confirmed and even married, and named and married in the same temple, Reform. And that's where I learned all the Hebrew. That's where I learned all my Hebrew prayers. Because it's English, and we understand what's going on.

INT: You mentioned your husband a moment ago. When and where did you meet him? And can you tell us?

VK: OK. Well, I was in San Francisco. I lived in San Francisco about six years. And then my cousin, who was in the-- well, he went to Israel and was in the Israeli navy, came to the United States. Also was in the US Army. And I wanted to see my cousin, whom I haven't seen since Shanghai, my one and only cousin, paternal cousin. So I came to New York for a visit on Labor Day in 1957. And there-- they belonged to some sort of a club where a lot of the Jewish people met. And my husband was there, too. He was a friend of his. And he was washing his car, et cetera, et cetera. And that's how I met my husband and a few other-- quite a few other people. And that's where I met my husband, with my cousin. And he was also going with a girl who was also a Holocaust survivor, my cousin was. OK? And he got married one year before we did, in the same place in New York.

INT: And was your husband also a survivor?

VK: My husband was definitely a survivor, yes.

INT: Can you share a little bit about his history?

VK: Well, he was born in Yugoslavia. And he was smuggled-- his father was taken away. And they found out later that he was killed. But he was smuggled into Italy as a girl. Because this woman lost her daughter, and he was smuggled into Italy as a girl. And his family also came. His mother and brother also entered Italy, and so did his extended family, his-- my mother-in-law's sister and her husband, and their children, and also extended cousins of theirs. So, oh, about 10 of us, or 10 of them were traveling in Italy, traveling in Italy. And an Italian, a very nice Italian, gave him a card and said we have an abandoned synagogue, and you can stay there. You know how the four corners and they had-- they raised crops, and they ate there, and they lived there in Rovigo. It's called Rovigo, I think, my-- for a couple of years. And they gave him ration cards and everything. And then he came one day and said the Germans are coming here, and you have to leave. But I have a brother in-- living in the Vatican in Rome. You can make yourself-- the whole-- you can all go to Rome. But don't use public transportation, because the Germans will ask you for ID. You have to go without them. So they went with bicycles and whatever transportation they found and made his way to Rome. And my husband went to school there and he lived in the Vatican with his cousin, with his one cousin who was about his age. The older one once went to another, different school, his brother and his other cousin. And they went to Vatican there. They were there a few years. And then President Roosevelt wanted to do something about getting the refugees in the United States. So they were the very first ones to come. And the war was not over yet. In 1944, by Ruth Gruber, who was sent to get them, if you are familiar with Ruth Gruber? She wrote that book, also, The Haven. That's the story of my husband, The Haven. The book is called The Haven. And she wrote many other books. And we went to many reunions and to her birthday parties, Ruth Gruber's birthday parties, at the many reunions when they were here, and they came to the States. They-- she took them-- the boat to the States. About 1,000 were allowed to come in, but only 800 came. Part of his family didn't want to come because they said that it's dangerous on the water because of the things they're exploding now. And the war was not over yet, you know. But some of them went to Brazil of his family. And the rest came to the United States, quite a few of them, their whole-- the 10 of them that were traveling together, they all came to the United States. And they were interned in Oswego, New York at Fort Ontario. And there is a museum there. And we had many reunions. I went with my husband. He went with my husband to his reunions, and he went to my reunions, Shanghai reunions. We went to a lot of reunions. And we saw the world this way. [LAUGHING] Anyway, and we went there. And he-- they were interned there. But the people in Oswego were wonderful. After school, the teachers taught-- came in to where they were interned, and they were in a fenced area. And in the United States was the first time they ever were in a fenced area. They were there. And the teachers came and taught them English. And after school, they were very good to them. But the older kids, like my husband's brother and his cousin, there was a hole in the fence. They got out of the fence and went with the bicycles to New York and ended up in New York in school. And they did a few other things. Nobody said anything, but they went out to school. He-- his cousin became a dentist here, was a very famous dentist. And his brother became an engineer. They all did very well. Anyway, once they were out-- OK. Let me-- I'm going to go back a little bit. Anyway, they had to sign a paper that when the war was over, they were going to leave. That's one of the other reasons why they were. So when Truman took over, Truman said I'm going to follow that you can leave. We're going to take-- we're going to take you to Canada. And you stay a day in Canada, and then you come back. So you'll be-- you fulfilled your reason, and then you can come and stay in the United States. You fulfilled that you had to leave. And then you can come and stay in the United States. Truman did that.

INT: What was your husband's name?

VK: William Kremer.

INT: What did you and your husband do to support your family? What work did you do?

VK: Well, my husband was a tool and die designer. So he worked there first as a tool and die designer. And I was a legal secretary. [PAUSES] When we first got married, that's where we were, what we were doing. And we lived in New Jersey at the time in a place-- they had little veterans' homes. It was very pretty. They were like little [INAUDIBLE] and in the middle, they had a nice lawn and all. And I worked in Hackensack, New Jersey. And after about three years, we bought a house. I had friends who lived in Orangeburg, New York, and we visited them. They were very-- she was very involved in the Holocaust, too, my friend. And we visited them. And then we bought a house in Pearl River. There was an opening in Pearl River, where they had a showing of one of the houses they were building, a new development. And we went there, and we decided to buy a house in Pearl River. So now-- I lived in Pearl River for 65 years. My husband passed away three years ago. And after, when we moved to Pearl River, my husband went and he was-- my daughter was-- my son was first born when we-- after we moved to Pearl River. And then two years later, my daughter was born. And my husband had a bad back. And he could-- he was flat on his back. And my mother-in-law got married again, for the third time. Her first husband, you know, was a Holocaust survivor. Her second husband she met in Oswego, and he passed away. And she got married again, the third time. [PAUSES] And he had a dry cleaning business. So my husband went into the dry cleaning business.

He went to school. And he became the dry cleaner. And he was in the dry cleaning business. They had, like, three stores in New York or something. And that's what he did until he retired. [PAUSES] And I continued-- I was not a legal secretary after that. I was an administrative assistant, and I worked in many different areas. And at the end, for the last 20 years, I worked in New York State right near me at Rockland Psychiatric Center as administrative secretary for the last 20 years.

INT: Have you spoken publicly about your experiences?

VK: Yes.

INT: Can you tell us about that?

VK: The first time both my husband and I spoke about both of our experiences was at The Holocaust Museum in Spring Valley. That was the first time that we both--

INT: When?

VK: Oh, that was a long time ago. It must have been, maybe, 30 years ago? That was a long time ago. Then I spoke at my temple to the Hebrew school kids and also at one time, at another time.

And a few years back, my husband was still alive, before-- about six years ago, I spoke in-- at a 55-plus community where a few of my friends moved into, was a brand new community, moved there from Temple. And I've been a member of that Beth Am Temple for 55 years. So anyway, I spoke there. [PAUSES] I spoke at another place once. I don't remember, a few, few times. OK? That's about all I can say. [PAUSES] And my suitcase, which I'll tell you, the traveling suitcase, my suitcase is at-- was-- I donated it to the museum, Spring Valley. And it's now-- the museum is now in Rockland Community College. OK? The whole museum, and I went there once. And I saw that they don't have my suitcase, but they have a picture of my suitcase and my name on it, and also the story of my husband. But I don't know, because it had my maiden name, that they did not know that I was the same person as that suitcase there. Because it had my maiden name on it, because that was-- the traveling suitcase has my maiden name, and I don't go anywhere with my maiden name. So I don't even know if the museum has my story, but they have my suitcase. [LAUGHING] I'm not sure about the story. Now a few of the cousins, of my husband's cousins, live in the Washington, DC, area, including my-- one of my niece for my husband's brother, the daughter, she lives in Arlington. And she comes and visits me quite often. And my husband's two children are my only niece and nephew, since I'm an only child, yeah? my husband's brother. My husband's brother had Alzheimer's for the last 10 years before he passed away. He passed away way before my-- he must be gone-- was it five, five or six years? My husband is gone three years, yeah. So [PAUSES] I went back to Shanghai on a reunion [PAUSES] in 2005. And that was a very, very nice experience. And they preserved the synagogue and the little part of our Shanghai-Jewish thing so that any visitor coming to Shanghai can see that part. Because a lot of people invested money, a lot of money into keeping that area. It's at the Ohel Synagogue, which became the museum. And I think my little report card is in that museum from the Bais Yaakov school. I'm not sure. [LAUGHING] But anyway, [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] that's all I can say. And then I went to see the rest of China, a little bit of the rest of China, which I never saw before. [BACKGROUND NOISE]

INT: Can you tell us a little bit about the Shanghailander community, the survivors from Shanghai, and how they stayed in touch all these years?

VK: Well, we had a lot of reunions. I had one in California, one in Philadelphia that I recall that I went-- that we went to. We didn't go to everything. But there were reunions all the time. They had a reunion in Israel, too. I didn't go to that one. I was in Israel three-- we were in Israel three times. The first time was in the '70s, when Bill's aunt and uncle, who were traveling with him to Oswego, decided to go to Israel, to live in Israel for a while. They're really from New York, but they decided live in Israel. And we were there at that time. At that time, it was a more luxurious trip, because we stayed at the King David Hotel, and all that. And it was a very pretty trip. The next trip we took with our daughter and family with our temple. We went with our temple to Israel. And there we saw the-- all the other areas, including the Holocaust there. And it was a very, very nice trip. My granddaughter was at that time almost 10 years old. And my husband was very excited to go and show her everything there. And then she was the youngest one of all the people there. And we were the oldest ones, my husband and I. I was turning 70 at the time. Then we went again with my son's family. My son married a Japanese girl, and he lives in Japan. And he has two boys, two sons who are Jewish. They're more religious than my kids here, that were here. [LAUGHING] My Japanese-Jewish boys, American-- they're also American citizens, because my son took them right to the embassy and told them that they were American. So they're both Japanese and American, the two boys. And they follow the Jewish religion. And the oldest one was studying in Israel at the Ben-Gurion University for his master's in cyber technology. And he wanted to stay in Israel. And he worked so hard to get a working visa. It took him a long time. And he finally got it. And then the war broke out in Israel. That was just now. Then the war broke out in Israel. So we didn't want him to stay there. We were afraid. So he